  "_comment": "Laniakea, the supercluster the Milky Way belongs to. Sources: the Wikipedia articles on the Laniakea Supercluster and the Virgo Supercluster. Laniakea runs about 520 Mly across (major axis 404 Mly), masses ~1e17 suns and holds ~100,000 galaxies in 300 to 500 known clusters and groups. It is not one lump: it is several constituent superclusters, and the Virgo Supercluster, the one we are in, is only a part of it. 'l' and 'b' are galactic longitude and latitude in degrees, 'mly' the distance in millions of light years, and 'position' the same thing as cartesian Mly (x toward the galactic centre, y toward l=90, z toward the north galactic pole). 'approx' marks an object placed in the right region at the right distance whose exact direction is NOT quotable; false means the direction is the real one, usually taken from the object's dominant galaxy. 'rich' is roughly how many galaxies it holds.",
